From c0600eeee5f9330470321f08bfbb50c794a672a4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: defcom5-rockchip <286489597+defcom5-rockchip@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 19:39:25 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] drm/rockchip: dw_hdmi_qp: hide HDMI 2.1 FRL modes unless
 allow_frl=1

On RK3588 (Orange Pi 5B) an HDMI 2.1 FRL link, e.g. 3840x2160@120,
hard-hangs the SoC whenever the link is torn down and rebuilt: screen
blank, sink HPD toggle, monitor power-off. TMDS modes up to 4K@60 are
unaffected. Until the FRL teardown/retrain path is fixed such modes must
not be offered to userspace by default, so GNOME's Displays panel lists
up to 4K@60 only.

Add an allow_frl module parameter (bool, default false, 0644). While it
is false dw_hdmi_rockchip_mode_valid() returns MODE_BAD for every mode
with mode->clock >= HDMI20_MAX_RATE (600000 kHz), independent of
enable_gpio and of dw_hdmi_qp_version, and prints one dev_info_once so
the missing 120 Hz entry is explained in dmesg.

The threshold is the pixel clock, not the TMDS character rate, and it is
">= HDMI20_MAX_RATE" rather than the "> 600000" of the existing
enable_gpio check:

 - hdmi_select_link_config() only stays on TMDS while
   (tmdsclk < HDMI20_MAX_RATE && mode.clock < HDMI20_MAX_RATE); it goes
   FRL for any mode.clock >= 600000 even when YUV420 halves the TMDS
   rate to 594 MHz. The board's monitor advertises 4K@120 in its
   YCbCr 4:2:0 capability map, so a check on the halved rate would let
   exactly the hanging mode through. Gating on mode->clock mirrors the
   driver's own FRL decision.
 - ">=" matches that decision exactly: a mode at precisely 600000 kHz
   would be driven over FRL by hdmi_select_link_config(), so it has to
   be hidden too. For the CEA timings that matter (594000 for 4K@60,
   1188000 for 4K@120) both comparisons behave the same.

The check sits after the existing enable_gpio / DW_HDMI_QP_V2 check so
the log line is only emitted when the knob is the reason a mode is
missing.

rockchipdrm is built in on this image, so the parameter is a boot
argument:

    rockchipdrm.allow_frl=1

(as a module: "options rockchipdrm allow_frl=1"). It can also be flipped
at runtime through /sys/module/rockchipdrm/parameters/allow_frl; the
mode list changes at the next connector probe (replug, or
"echo detect > /sys/class/drm/card*-HDMI-A-1/status").

Not covered: mode_valid() does not know the colour depth, so a
sub-600 MHz mode requested at 10-bit RGB/YUV444 on an HDMI 2.1 sink
(TMDS rate x1.25, see the link_cfg.rate_per_lane branch in the output
format selection) can still end up on FRL. The default output is 8-bit
(or whatever U-Boot lit the display with), so reaching that path needs
an explicit color_depth property change.

Written with the help of an LLM assistant (Claude); the hang report and
the decision to gate FRL by default are the submitter's.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/dw_hdmi_qp-rockchip.c | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/dw_hdmi_qp-rockchip.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/dw_hdmi_qp-rockchip.c
index 94c06bcc12fc..4f8f012d2f7f 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/dw_hdmi_qp-rockchip.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/dw_hdmi_qp-rockchip.c
@@ -43,6 +43,10 @@ static unsigned int frl_max_rate_per_lane;
 module_param(frl_max_rate_per_lane, uint, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(frl_max_rate_per_lane, "Cap the HDMI FRL rate per lane in Gbps (3/6/8/10/12, 0 = no cap)");
 
+static bool allow_frl;
+module_param(allow_frl, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(allow_frl, "Offer HDMI 2.1 FRL modes (pixel clock >= 600 MHz) to userspace (default: false)");
+
 #define HIWORD_UPDATE(val, mask)	(val | (mask) << 16)
 
 #define RK3538_VO_GRF_HDMI_MISC		0x68
@@ -1728,6 +1732,19 @@ dw_hdmi_rockchip_mode_valid(struct dw_hdmi *dw_hdmi, void *data,
 	    mode->clock > 600000)
 		return MODE_BAD;
 
+	/*
+	 * Tearing down and rebuilding an FRL link (blank, sink HPD toggle,
+	 * monitor power-off) hard-hangs the RK3588 SoC. Until that is fixed,
+	 * hide every mode hdmi_select_link_config() would drive over FRL,
+	 * i.e. pixel clock >= HDMI20_MAX_RATE even for YUV420 (whose TMDS
+	 * character rate is halved), unless the user opts in with allow_frl.
+	 */
+	if (!allow_frl && mode->clock >= HDMI20_MAX_RATE) {
+		dev_info_once(hdmi->dev, "hiding HDMI 2.1 FRL modes (%dx%d@%d, %d kHz); set rockchipdrm.allow_frl=1 to offer them\n",
+			      mode->hdisplay, mode->vdisplay, drm_mode_vrefresh(mode), mode->clock);
+		return MODE_BAD;
+	}
+
 	return MODE_OK;
 }
 
